{{also|Bask}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|baðask||to take a bath; literally to bathe oneself, with the suffix ''-sk'' representing Old Norse ''[[sik]]'' (oneself)}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/bɑːsk/|/bæsk/|lang=en}}
* {{enPR|băsk}}, {{IPA|/bæsk/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-bask.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|Basque}} (in some dialects)
* {{rhymes|æsk|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To bathe in warmth; to be exposed to pleasant heat.
#: {{ux|en|to '''bask''' in the sun}}
#* Goldsmith
#*: {{...}} '''basks''' in the glare, and stems the tepid wave.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To take great pleasure or satisfaction; to feel warmth or happiness. (This verb is usually followed by "in").
#: {{ux|en|I '''basked''' in her love.}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''bask''' in someone's favour}}
#* {{quote-journal|date=November 7, 2012|author=Matt Bai|title=Winning a Second Term, Obama Will Confront Familiar Headwinds|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html?pagewanted=all&_r=0
|passage=As President Obama turns his attention once again to filling out a cabinet and writing an Inaugural Address, this much is clear: he should not expect to '''bask''' in a surge of national unity, or to witness a crowd of millions overrun the Mall just to say they were there.}}
#* {{quote-journal|date=April 10, 2011
|author=Alistair Magowan
|title=Aston Villa 1 - 0 Newcastle
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9446263.stm
|page=
|passage=On this evidence they will certainly face tougher tests, as a depleted Newcastle side seemed to '''bask''' in the relative security of being ninth in the table}}

